  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "A runner moves along a straight track relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=3{t}^{2}-2t-12,$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. The positive direction is left to right. If the runner is stationary when $t=4\\,\\text{s},$ determine the time interval in which the runner moves to the left.\n\n{{field-1}}\n\n$<t<$\n\n{{field-2}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3{t}^{2}-2t-12)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{3}-{t}^{2}-12t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(4)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =(4)^{3}-(4)^{2}-12(4)+C \\\\ 0 & =64-16-48+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the runner at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{3}-{t}^{2}-12t.$\n\nTo find the time interval in which the runner is moving to the *left*, we must solve the inequality $v<0.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}-12t=t(t-4)(t+3)<0.\n$$\n\nPlotting the graph of $v(t)$, we get the following picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/assets/27b9a41b0ad5c3ff98f6d76e24f0dc74e863d8f63a4c033fc41b7f7f2ebd72ea.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $v<0$ whenever $0\\,<\\,t\\,<\\,4.$",
